WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:07.640
Thankfully, one of the biggest scams in the
American tax code

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:12.360
is finally under attack in the House version
of Republican tax reform.

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:13.960
It's the mortgage-interest deduction,

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:17.060
which currently lets homeowners deduct interest
paid on mortgages

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:19.760
of up to $1 million for two houses.

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:27.580
Ever since owning a home has been a central
tenet of the American Dream

00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:30.600
since the end of World War II and the rise
of suburbia,

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:33.810
it's been a given that deducting mortgage
interest from your taxes

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:35.900
is as American as apple pie.

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:42.500
The House plan would limit filers to deducting
interest on the first $500,000

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:44.420
of a mortgage on just one house,

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:48.960
sending a blind panic through wealthy home
owners, realtors, and the building trades,

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:53.190
all of whom are terrified that a government
subsidy is being yanked away from them.

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:58.480
We are very concerned with the limitation
on the mortgage interest deduction

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:00.140
and it's really limited in two ways-

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:04.030
But the real problem with the House bill is
that it doesn't go far enough.

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:06.920
We should scrap the mortgage-interest deduction
altogether

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:10.620
and let housing prices reflect real market
values.

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:14.480
The mortgage-interest deduction is typically
justified by claiming that it lets people,

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:21.760
especially vaguely defined "middle-class"
people, afford homes.

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:23.900
But it also increases the price of housing

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:26.300
by making it artificially cheap to borrow money,

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:28.740
meaning homebuyers are willing to borrow more.

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:33.720
England, Canada, and Australia don't let their
taxpayers deduct their mortgage interest

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:37.200
and they all have higher rates of homeownership
than the United States.

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:42.860
The mortgage-interest deduction disproportionately
benefits the wealthiest Americans,

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:48.750
who soak up almost all the $70 billion a year
it costs in foregone revenue each year.

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:51.700
The tax bill, both the House and the Senate
versions,

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:54.410
both of them leave alone, for the most part,
the mortgage interest deduction,

00:01:54.410 --> 00:01:56.960
which is one of the most ridiculous parts
of the tax code.

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:00.440
Reason Foundation's director of economic research,
Anthony Randazzo

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:04.890
calculates that only 20 percent of tax filers
claim the mortgage-interest deduction.

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:07.580
That group by and large are part of six-figure
households

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:11.980
in a country where the median household income
is just $57,000.

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:14.940
Killing the mortgage-interest deduction

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:18.740
might cause a one-time 7 percent drop in real estate prices,

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:23.220
according to one estimate, with wealthy homeowners
feeling most of the pain.

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:25.600
Speaking as a homeowner,

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:30.470
that seems like a small price to pay to end
a policy that distorts the real estate market,

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:34.860
complicates the tax code, and benefits mostly
wealthier Americans

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:39.420
all on the false promise that it makes home-owning
affordable for the middle class.

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:43.500
The mortgage-interest deduction is just special-interest
pandering

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:48.060
wrapped in a gooey story that equates "the
American Dream" with having a mortgage.

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:51.000
The tax code should be designed to raise the
revenue necessary

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:53.000
to pay for essential services,

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:55.330
not to nudge and prod us into spending money

00:02:55.330 --> 00:02:58.280
on something the government decides is good
for us.

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:01.950
The House tax reform plan would be at least
a good down payment

00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:04.060
on a more libertarian tax code.

